# Line endings.
#
# Everything committed here is already LF in the index — the repository has
# always been consistent. What was missing was anything telling git to keep the
# working tree that way, so a Windows editor rewriting a file with CRLF left it
# permanently "modified" against an identical blob. The symptom was every file
# in the repo showing as changed with no change in it, and `gh` warning about a
# hundred uncommitted files on every push.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises on the way in and checks out LF everywhere,
# including on Windows. Nothing in this project needs CRLF: the toolchain is
# Node, the CI is Linux, and Windows editors have handled LF for years.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Never touched, in either direction. `text=auto` guesses these correctly from
# their contents, but a guess is not worth relying on for the ones we know.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.pdf binary

# CRLF here is not cosmetic — it is the difference between a script that runs
# and `bad interpreter: no such file or directory`.
*.sh text eol=lf
